5a. Attribution — where the ideas and templates came from
---------------------------------------------------------
Almost everything in this repository was programmed by the author, but it
would not exist without the open-source ecosystem. In the spirit of honest
attribution, the sources of inspiration, design templates and provider
concepts are named explicitly:

* VLC / libVLC (VideoLAN, LGPL-2.1-or-later / GPL-2.0-or-later): the desktop
  player's feature model (track menus, EPG concepts, stream handling,
  snapshot/A-B/equalizer ideas) and the in-process playback architecture were
  studied from VLC. libVLC is used unmodified as a system runtime dependency.
* Webamp (Jordan Eldredge / contributors, MIT license) and the classic
  Winamp look: the red/dark skin language, visualizer aesthetics and the
  compact player layout of MPCASU's web and desktop UI were inspired by
  Webamp's Winamp-style design. No Webamp code is copied.
* yt-dlp (yt-dlp contributors, The Unlicense): the YouTube provider —
  metadata search and stream URL resolution — follows yt-dlp's documented
  CLI behavior; yt-dlp is invoked as an external tool, never vendored.
* spotDL (spotDL contributors, MIT license): the Spotify provider concept —
  Spotify metadata matched onto YouTube audio sources (`spotdl save`,
  `spotdl url`) — is realized through spotDL as an external, optional tool.

No source code from VLC, Webamp, Winamp, yt-dlp, spotDL or any other
third-party project is copied, vendored or derived in this repository — all
CASU and MPCASU source files are original works of the author. VLC, Winamp
and Spotify are trademarks of their respective owners; no affiliation or
endorsement is implied. Runtime dependencies (libVLC, FFmpeg, PyAV, PySide6,
yt-dlp, spotDL and others) remain separately licensed, unmodified external
components; users must comply with their licenses (see
THIRD_PARTY_COMPONENTS.md).

